Just throwing a thought here - I would like to hear from API approvers first before we make any changes (as this field is already there in beta APIs).

But my question is - do we really need this field? Technically, its value can be fully inferred from the fields below (exactly one of the below ones should be set and the value of this field is the the name of corresponding type).

@thockin - I guess there might be reasons for having it that I don't see?

Explicit discriminators make it very clear what is happening when there is version skew.  E.g. your controller doesn't know about option X, but the API does.  The controller gets either `{}` or `{type: "X"}`.  In one case you don't know if nothing was specified (error?) or if something was specified but you don't understand it.

I think it's OK to default the discriminator from the content, as long as apiserver knows the new option.

@apelisse did a lot of thinking here

looking at the delta between v2beta2 and v2 types.go:

```diff
+       // PodResourceMetricSourceType is a resource metric known to Kubernetes, as
-       // ResourceMetricSourceType is a resource metric known to Kubernetes, as
        // specified in requests and limits, describing each pod in the current
        // scale target (e.g. CPU or memory).  Such metrics are built in to
        // Kubernetes, and have special scaling options on top of those available
        // to normal per-pod metrics (the "pods" source).
+       PodResourceMetricSourceType MetricSourceType = "PodResource"
-       ResourceMetricSourceType MetricSourceType = "Resource"
```

it looks like this is trying to change the valid enum values for this type field... that seems confusing to me and will require special handling in conversion (a v1/v2beta1/v2beta2 object that sets `type=Resource` would have to automatically convert to a v2 object that sets `type=PodResource`). Is that conversion being done? Is it worth the complexity/difficulty of changing this value?
